          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Right Explanation / ELI6 vs Exam Column -->
      <div class="lg:w-5/12 flex flex-col gap-4">
        <!-- Kid Friendly Story Box (Shown in ELI6 Mode) -->
        <div id="card-basics-eli6" class="mode-view-eli6 bg-gradient-to-br from-emerald-950/40 via-slate-900 to-slate-900 p-5 rounded-2xl border border-emerald-500/30 relative">
          <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-2">
            <span class="text-2xl">🏴‍☠️</span>
            <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-emerald-300 eli6-font tracking-wide">Explain Like I'm 6: The Pirate & The Flashlight</h3>
          </div>
          <div class="text-sm text-slate-300 space-y-2 leading-relaxed">
            <p>
              Imagine standing on a pirate island right at the center spot (called the <strong class="text-cyan-300">Pole</strong> or Origin $O$).
            </p>
            <p>
              In normal school math (<strong>Cartesian</strong>), you tell a robot: <em>"Walk 3 steps right, then 4 steps forward."</em> Like walking through square city blocks!
            </p>
            <p>
              In <strong class="text-emerald-300">Polar math</strong>, you are a pirate holding a flashlight! 
              First, you <strong class="text-emerald-300">spin your feet by an angle $\theta$</strong> (starting from the initial line).
              Then you simply <strong class="text-cyan-300">walk straight in that direction for distance $r$</strong>!
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="mt-4 p-3 bg-slate-950/70 rounded-xl border border-slate-800 text-xs space-y-1.5">
            <div class="font-semibold text-emerald-400">The Magic Triangle Bridge:</div>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 gap-2 text-slate-300 math-font">
              <div class="bg-slate-900 p-2 rounded border border-slate-800">
                $x = r \cos\theta$
              </div>
              <div class="bg-slate-900 p-2 rounded border border-slate-800">
                $y = r \sin\theta$
              </div>
              <div class="bg-slate-900 p-2 rounded border border-slate-800 col-span-2">
                $x^2 + y^2 = r^2$ (Pythagoras theorem!)
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Rigorous Cambridge Exam Card (Shown in Exam Mode) -->
        <div id="card-basics-exam" class="mode-view-exam hidden bg-gradient-to-br from-cyan-950/40 via-slate-900 to-slate-900 p-5 rounded-2xl border border-cyan-500/40 relative">
          <div class="flex items-center justify-between mb-2">
            <div class="flex items-center gap-2">
              <span class="text-xl">🎓</span>
              <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-cyan-300 tracking-wide">Cambridge 9231 Syllabus Spec: Section 5.1</h3>
            </div>
            <span class="text-[10px] px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-cyan-500/20 text-cyan-300 font-mono">Paper 1 Core</span>
          </div>

          <div class="text-xs text-slate-300 space-y-2.5 leading-relaxed">
            <div class="p-2.5 rounded-lg bg-slate-950/80 border border-slate-800">
              <span class="text-amber-300 font-semibold block mb-1">Key Definition & Coordinates Conversion:</span>
              $$\begin{cases} x = r \cos\theta \\ y = r \sin\theta \end{cases} \iff \begin{cases} r^2 = x^2 + y^2 \quad (r \ge 0) \\ \tan\theta = \frac{y}{x} \end{cases}$$
            </div>

            <div class="space-y-1">
              <span class="font-semibold text-cyan-400 flex items-center gap-1.5">
                <span>⚠️</span> Common Cambridge Marking Traps:
              </span>
              <ul class="list-disc pl-4 space-y-1 text-slate-300 text-[11px]">
                <li><strong>The $r \ge 0$ Mandate:</strong> CIE 9231 requires $r \ge 0$. Any interval where $f(\theta) < 0$ is rejected from the curve domain.</li>
                <li><strong>Initial Line:</strong> Always defined along the Cartesian positive $x$-axis ($\theta = 0$). Anti-clockwise is positive $\theta$.</li>
                <li><strong>Cartesian vs. Polar Intersections:</strong> Curves may both pass through the pole $O$ at <em>different</em> angles $\theta$. In Cartesian coordinates they share $(0,0)$, but their polar coordinate pairs are distinct!</li>
              </ul>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Mode-Adaptive Key Rules Card -->
        <div class="bg-slate-900/60 p-4 rounded-2xl border border-slate-800 text-xs space-y-2">
          <div class="flex items-center justify-between text-amber-400 font-bold uppercase tracking-wider text-[11px]">
            <span class="flex items-center gap-1.5">
              <span>⭐</span> <span id="rules-title">Pirate Quick Rules</span>
            </span>
            <span id="rules-tag" class="text-[10px] text-slate-400 lowercase font-mono">eli6</span>
          </div>
          <div id="rules-content" class="text-slate-300 text-xs leading-relaxed">
            <!-- Dynamically populated based on mode -->
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- TAB 2: CURVE ZOO & PAINTER ENGINE -->
    <section id="tab-curves" class="tab-panel hidden flex flex-col lg:flex-row gap-6">
      <div class="lg:w-7/12 flex flex-col gap-3">
