            </label>
            <input type="range" id="slider-area-beta" min="0.1" max="6.28" step="0.05" value="3.141" class="w-full">
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- ELI6 Area derivation and textbook questions -->
      <div class="lg:w-5/12 flex flex-col gap-4">
        <div class="bg-gradient-to-br from-amber-950/30 via-slate-900 to-slate-900 p-5 rounded-2xl border border-amber-500/30">
          <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-2">
            <span class="text-2xl">🍕</span>
            <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-amber-300 eli6-font">Why Not Rectangles? The Pizza Slice Story!</h3>
          </div>
          <div class="text-sm text-slate-300 space-y-2 leading-relaxed">
            <p>
              In regular calculus with $x$ and $y$, you find areas by stacking millions of super-skinny tall <strong>rectangular Lego blocks</strong> ($\text{Height } y \times \text{Width } dx$).
            </p>
            <p>
              Under polar coordinates, our laser spins in a circle! So rectangles would look all crooked and bumpy.
            </p>
            <p>
              Instead, you slice a round <strong>giant pizza into super tiny wedges</strong>! 
            </p>
            <div class="bg-slate-950/80 p-3 rounded-xl border border-slate-800 text-xs text-slate-200">
              The area of one tiny triangular pizza slice with radius $r$ and angle width $d\theta$ is:
              <div class="text-center font-bold text-amber-400 text-sm py-1">
                $\delta A \approx \frac{1}{2} \cdot r^2 \cdot \delta\theta$
              </div>
